The product of effectively given neighbourhood systems is effectively given, and the product combinators are computable.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`prodEnum`).** The product enumeration pairs an index from each factor and returns the corresponding product neighbourhood.

**Theorem (`prodEnum_apply`).** The neighbourhood at a paired product index is the product of the two neighbourhoods at its component indices.

*Proof.* Both product coordinates reduce to the corresponding facts in the two factor presentations. ∎

**Theorem (`prodNbhd_eq_iff`).** Two product neighbourhoods are equal exactly when their left components and their right components are respectively equal.

*Proof.* Both product coordinates reduce to the corresponding facts in the two factor presentations. ∎

**Definition (`prodPresentation`).** Pairing the two factor enumerations gives a computable presentation of the product neighbourhood system.

**Theorem (`prodPresentation_X`).** The product presentation enumerates the product of the neighbourhoods named by the two components of an index.

*Proof.* Both product coordinates reduce to the corresponding facts in the two factor presentations. ∎

**Theorem (`paired_isComputable`).** Pairing two computable approximable maps with common source gives a computable map into the product.

*Proof.* The required relation is obtained from recursively enumerable or decidable index relations by pairing, composition, conjunction, and projection. ∎

**Theorem (`prod_isEffectivelyGiven`).** The product of two effectively given neighbourhood systems is effectively given.

*Proof.* Both product coordinates reduce to the corresponding facts in the two factor presentations. ∎

**Theorem (`proj₀_isComputable`).** The first projection from an effectively presented product is computable.

*Proof.* The required relation is obtained from recursively enumerable or decidable index relations by pairing, composition, conjunction, and projection. ∎

**Theorem (`proj₁_isComputable`).** The second projection from an effectively presented product is computable.

*Proof.* The required relation is obtained from recursively enumerable or decidable index relations by pairing, composition, conjunction, and projection. ∎

**Theorem (`prodMap_isComputable`).** The product of two computable approximable maps is computable.

*Proof.* The required relation is obtained from recursively enumerable or decidable index relations by pairing, composition, conjunction, and projection. ∎
